B1.3 – Prove Runtime Binding (2 Marks)
When virtual is removed from total(), runtime polymorphism is lost.

Since Bill* is used to access the derived objects, the compiler cannot automatically call the derived total() function through the base pointer.

If total() is changed to a normal non-virtual function, the base-class function is called instead of the derived function. If the base function is not defined, compilation may fail.

Conclusion: Virtual functions allow the correct derived-class function to be selected at runtime.

B1.4 – Prove the Virtual Destructor (2 Marks)
The original code contains:
virtual ~Bill() = default;


If virtual is removed:
~Bill() = default;


Deleting derived objects through a Bill* causes undefined behaviour because the derived destructor is not guaranteed to execute.

Run using AddressSanitizer:
g++ -std=c++17 -Wall -Wextra -fsanitize=address -g main.cpp -o main
./main


Observation: Removing the virtual destructor causes undefined behaviour. The derived destructor may not execute correctly, which can lead to resource leaks. AddressSanitizer may or may not detect this particular issue.

Conclusion: A virtual destructor is necessary when deleting derived objects through base-class pointers.

Alternative Concept and Complexity Analysis
If templates were used instead of virtual functions, each bill type could be stored in a separate container and processed using compile-time polymorphism.

Example:
template <typename T>
void processBills(T bills[]) {
    for (int i = 0; i < 2; i++) {
        std::cout << bills[i].total() << '\n';
    }
}


Feature	Virtual Functions	Templates
Time per bill	O(1)	O(1)
Virtual call overhead	Present	Absent
Space	Vtable pointer per object	No vtable required
Containers	Can store different bill types using base pointers	Usually separate containers for each type
Flexibility	Different bill types in one list	Difficult to store mixed types in one list

Conclusion: Templates can be faster because they avoid virtual function calls, but virtual functions are more suitable when the hospital must store and process different bill types together in one collection.
